Revision history for Maat

0.06    Unreleased
        - Maat::MCP::Tools: the tools a proxied document offers an agent,
          derived once at compile time from the same Open::API object the
          gateway enforces. Needs Open::API 0.13 for operation_schema and
          operation_doc.
        - Maat::MCP: an MCP endpoint at /mcp on the gateway itself, off
          unless a tenant enables it. One JSON-RPC message per POST:
          initialize, ping, tools/list, tools/call. tools/list answers
          from bytes frozen at compile time. A tools/call IS a request
          through the gateway: it re-enters the same C entry a customer's
          own client reaches, so it is matched, validated, metered and
          forwarded exactly once. Arguments are checked against the tool
          schema first, so a refused call costs the tenant nothing.
        - Maat::MCP authenticates. The tenant's own API keys are bearer
          tokens at /mcp, hashed and compared in constant time against
          the keys already on the environment, so there is no second
          lookup and a revoked key dies with the next bundle. A key needs
          the `mcp` scope, or no scopes at all. Missing or unknown is 401
          with a WWW-Authenticate naming the metadata document; a key
          without the scope is 403. A tenant publishing no keys is open,
          the same stance the API path takes.
        - The RFC 9728 protected-resource document is served at
          /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource, built once per tenant
          and public, because it is what tells a client how to
          authenticate. It names an authorization server only when the
          tenant is configured for OAuth.
        - A tool call carries the key the endpoint resolved, so it is
          rate limited and metered per key like any other request, and
          _authorize trusts it rather than re-checking a credential the
          gateway never stored.
        - OAuth 2.1 access tokens at /mcp, verified statelessly against
          the authorization server's public keys. The registry bundle
          carries a top-level `jwks`, held once per worker rather than
          per tenant and replaced wholesale by each poll, so a key
          rotation lands on the next bundle without recompiling a
          tenant. A token must be signed by one of those keys, name this
          gateway's issuer, carry this endpoint's canonical resource as
          its audience, be in date, hold the `mcp` scope and name this
          tenant. Its `apip_key` claim resolves against the tenant's
          published keys, so revoking a key kills a live token on the
          next bundle, and the client's token never goes upstream.
        - A tenant configured for OAuth whose keys are all revoked now
          refuses every request rather than falling back to open. A
          keys-only tenant with no keys is still open, as the API path
          is.
        - apip gains `mcp`: `apip mcp PROXY` shows whether the endpoint
          is on and whether it is actually being served, `on`/`off`
          toggle it (with `--ops` choosing which operations are offered
          as tools), and `tools` lists what an agent would be shown.
        - apip gains `examples`, reaching the mock examples the console
          already offered: list them, set one from a file or stdin,
          remove one. With `mcp` this closes the last operations the
          published document declared that no command could reach, so
          t/27-cli-parity.t passes whole.
        - Tool calls are metered. A tools/call re-enters the gateway, so
          it was already billed as the request it is; a new `mcp`
          counter beside `request` says how many of an hour's requests
          an agent made, and both totals ride in the SAME usage entry
          because the control plane replaces that row rather than adding
          to it. tools/list, initialize and ping are not metered: a
          client lists on every session it opens, and billing that would
          charge a customer for an agent connecting.
        - An MCP-only rate limit: at most 300 tool calls per key per
          minute across every tool, overridable per key with
          `mcp_calls_per_min`. The per-operation limit buckets by (key,
          operation), so an agent calling sixty different cheap tools
          once a second tripped none of them. A refused call is answered
          as a tool error carrying `_meta.retry_after`, so the model
          waits instead of retrying in a loop, and it never becomes a
          request.
        - tools/list is paged at 50 tools, with an opaque cursor and no
          cursor on the last page. The pages are frozen when the tenant
          compiles, exactly as the single list was, so a document with
          four hundred operations costs a client one more round trip and
          this gateway nothing per request. A tenant with fewer than 50
          tools answers byte for byte what it did before.
        - A violation records the tool an agent called. Attribution
          only: `tool` is deliberately not part of the fingerprint,
          because the same drift reached through two tools is one drift
          in the upstream.
        - Requires Reverse::Proxy 0.06, raised from 0.03. 0.04 is the
          release that re-encodes the forwarded request target, without
          which a client URL carrying %0d%0a reached the upstream as a
          real CRLF and started a second, attacker-written request on
          that connection: request smuggling past everything this
          gateway enforces.
        - `apip-fleet check` says which tenants answer /mcp and how an
          agent authenticates to them. It reads the bundle, so a proxy
          switched on below the plan that carries it is reported off,
          which is what it will be.
        - bench/mcp.pl: what the agent endpoint costs. Turning MCP on
          does not move the API path (within noise on a 30,000 iteration
          best-of-7), and a tool call costs ~4.4us over the same request
          made directly. bench/RESULTS.md carries the block.
        - FIX: the first MCP request to reach a fleet through the
          dispatcher warned "uninitialized value", because Maat::Router
          seeded every counter the C increments except `mcp`. Nothing
          caught it: every MCP test drove the endpoint directly rather
          than the route to it, which is now covered in t/20-router.t.
        - FIX: the request-validation path could corrupt the Perl stack
          on perls before 5.32. When validate_env returned exactly one
          value, mt_serve read it with SvTRUE(POPs); SvTRUE evaluated its
          argument more than once until 5.32, so POPs fired once per
          expansion and walked the stack pointer below the stack base.
          The two-value branch beside it already popped into a temporary
          and was correct. Found by sweeping every dist for the pattern
          after the same bug crashed Punk::OpenTelemetry 0.03 on a
          smoker.
        Phase 10 of plan_maat, the gateway half: the docs portal. Published
        documentation rendered from the SAME compiled spec the gateway is
        enforcing, so the docs and the runtime cannot disagree - one
        artifact is documented, enforced and measured. Off by default: with
        no `portal` in the gateway config there is no /docs route at all,
        and the request path is the phase 1-9 path byte for byte.

        - Maat::Portal: a per-tenant/host docs site served at /docs on the
          gateway itself. Rendered ONCE, at compile time, off the request
          path in Maat::Registry - the generated reference (Open::API::UI),
          the prose guides (Markdown::Simple), the per-operation client
          snippets and the Search::Trigram index are all boot work. A
          request into /docs is then a hash lookup into frozen bytes, not a
          template run: the `renders` counter does not move on a request.
        - The spec version and its content hash ride on every page, so a
          reader always knows which contract they are reading. The version
          and digest are exactly the pair Maat::Registry holds and the
          router stamps on X-Maat-Version, so the docs and the enforced
          spec move together - change the spec and both move (gate 1).
        - Prose guides fetched lazily from their revision, the exact mirror
          of the spec fetch: Maat::Registry gains `fetch_guides` (a coderef
          proxy-id -> { rev, guides }), called only when a tenant's
          portal.guides_rev is non-empty, cached per proxy. A guides_rev or
          badges change with the spec digest unchanged rebuilds only that
          tenant's frozen portal, off the request path, on its next request.
        - Search across the reference and the prose through Search::Trigram,
          the index built at boot from the same frozen content and queried
          at /docs/search.
        - The reality badge, the differentiator: per operation, a badge
          from THIS gateway's own local drift aggregates. Maat::Violation
          gains a standing per-operation `drift` view (not cleared on
          flush) that records which operations' responses have violated
          their schema; the badge reads exactly that, so it equals what the
          pipeline holds by construction, and because the same aggregates
          ship to the control plane it reconciles with the control plane's
          drift view (gate 3). Gated by the tenant's portal.badges opt-in:
          off, no badge is ever rendered and /docs/reality.json reports
          nothing.
        - Generated client snippets per operation - curl and
          Open::API::Client - from the compiled spec, with path templates
          filled from the declared parameter schemas, curl highlighted
          through Eshu.
        - Maat::Config gains a `portal` key (default off, the gate) and
          Maat::Registry a `portal` switch and `fetch_guides` fetcher. With
          the portal off the tenant app is returned bare - no wrapper, no
          /docs route, not one added op on the API request path (gate 4).
        - t/27-portal.t: 63 tests covering the four gates and the portal's
          own surface. `prove -lb t/` is 836 tests green.

        FIX: the spec fetcher wedged a worker instead of waiting. It
        built a loop-less Fetch agent, which inside a Hyperman worker
        puts the request's fd on a private standalone loop while
        Fetch::Future->get pumps the ACTIVE one - so the request is never
        registered with the loop being pumped, never completes, and its
        timeout never fires either, because the timer is on the abandoned
        loop too. Maat::Poller::_ua carries this warning already; the
        fetcher was the next thing to hit it.

        It presented as a fleet that worked and then did not: a tenant
        compiled earlier kept serving, a tenant whose FIRST request
        landed there never answered at all, and every worker looked
        healthy in ps because a wedged one spins a loop rather than
        blocking in a syscall. The agent now shares the worker's loop,
        and is rebuilt when the pid changes - it is created in the parent
        and inherited, so one made before the fork shared its socket with
        every sibling, which Maat::Poller::arm has always guarded against
        and this did not.

        And the fleet now says what it is doing, which is why the above
        took an evening to find rather than a minute.

        - Maat::Log: one JSON object per line on STDERR, where both
          supervisors already look. Off unless enabled, so a library
          caller writes nothing uninvited; never dies and never warns,
          because every call site is a failure path and an emitter that
          throws inside an error handler turns a bad minute into an
          outage.
        - The events are the ones that were missing when they were
          wanted: `compile` start and its terminator with a duration (a
          start with neither `ok` nor `fail` beside it is a compile that
          never came back), `poison` with the reason, `bundle` as an
          added/removed diff rather than a total, `fetch` failures with
          the URL, `poll` for every result except not_modified - four
          workers on a fifteen-second timer would otherwise write a line
          every four seconds saying nothing happened.
        - The per-request facts stay counters. A line per unknown host is
          a log-flooding lever any stranger can pull with a made-up
          hostname.
        - `stats_dir` and `apip-fleet stats`: each worker writes its
          counters to a file, atomically, at most once an interval. A
          file rather than an endpoint because this fleet serves
          arbitrary customer hostnames - any metrics PATH would shadow
          some tenant's real route - and because a file is still readable
          when a worker is too busy to answer HTTP. Per worker and never
          summed: they keep their own registries and poll on their own
          timers, and when they disagree the command says so, which is
          the shape of a worker still serving its boot-time tenant table.
          A worker that stopped reporting is shown stale, not counted.
        - `apip-fleet check` names the cost of running without either.

        A certificate issued after the fleet started is now served the
        poll after it is issued, rather than the restart after. It was
        the last manual step in an otherwise automatic custom-domain
        lifecycle, and an invisible one: the domain verified, issued,
        routed and read "live" in the console while the customer's
        client still failed the handshake.

        - Maat::Registry tracks a digest of each domain's certificate
          bytes and bumps tls_generation when the set changes - a domain
          added, removed, or RENEWED. A renewal is the same host at the
          same derived path with different bytes, so a path comparison
          would have missed the case that matters most. `on_tls_change`
          is called with the new SNI map when it moves and not
          otherwise, which is what stops a stable fleet rebuilding an
          SSL context every fifteen seconds for nothing.
        - Maat::Fleet wires that to Hyperman->tls_reload (0.15), which
          rebuilds the worker's context in place: nothing unbound, no
          connection refused, each worker picking it up as its own poll
          comes round. Wired only with a TLS listener of this fleet's
          own and only when the installed Hyperman has the call, so a
          balancer deployment costs nothing and an older Hyperman
          degrades to needing the restart. `tls_reload: false` in
          fleet.yml turns it off.

        Maat::Fleet, and with it a hosted data plane that exists. Phases
        6 and 7 left Registry, Poller, Router and Shard as four tested
        libraries with nothing owning them in a process, so
        <slug>.openapi-proxy.com answered nothing at all. This is the
        composition, and bin/apip-fleet and bin/fleet_server are its
        front doors.

        - The parent polls once, synchronously, before anything binds,
          because Hyperman builds each listener's SSL_CTX before it
          forks and the custom-domain certificates come out of that
          poll. Each worker then arms the poller on its own loop from
          its first request. A tenant, spec or route is live within one
          poll; a certificate issued after start is served on the next
          RESTART, which is why the shipped systemd unit does not wire
          SIGHUP to `systemctl reload`.
        - boot_poll returns the status word and never dies: a running
          fleet survives a failed poll on its last good bundle, a
          starting one has nothing to fall back to, and only the caller
          knows which it is. apip-fleet retries and then refuses.
        - The refusals are at boot, not at the first request: a pool
          nothing hashes to is named against the pools that exist,
          `pools` without `pool` is refused, and tls_dir is created 0700
          before a bundle can unseal a customer's private key into it.
        - t/32-fleet.t drives a fleet against a control plane on a real
          socket, so the registry URLs, the Bearer token on a lazy spec
          fetch and the arming are exercised rather than injected past.

        Plan-tier response sampling, the data-plane half. The control
        plane has always carried a per-plan sampling rate in the bundle
        and this end has always ignored it, so every hosted tenant ran
        adaptive whatever it paid for.

        - Maat::Config->tenant applies the bundle's `sampling` to
          responses.sample, and clamps it against limits.sampling first -
          the same shape as the enforce gate beside it, so what a tenant
          may have is decided from its plan rather than from the value
          that arrived next to it. Config->load is untouched, so a
          self-hosted apip.yml still says exactly what it means.
        - Maat::Sampler: the floor now applies in fixed mode. `sample: N`
          was a divisor nothing could lower, so 1-in-100 against an
          operation serving two requests an hour validated one response
          every fifty hours - a coverage report that reads empty rather
          than clean. Fixed is a ceiling now, and the floor may lower it.
          THIS CHANGES BEHAVIOUR for a self-hosted gateway with an
          explicit `sample: N`: at low traffic it validates more than it
          used to, which is what `floor` always said it would do.
        - Maat::Sampler->retune advances its clock in fixed and off mode
          instead of returning early without touching it. The request
          path guards the call with next_at and builds the coverage hash
          to pass in, so a next_at that never moved turned a
          once-an-interval 0.5us into a per-response one.

0.05    Unreleased
        Phase 9 of plan_maat, the data-plane half: API keys, per-key rate
        limits and the abuse ceilings that keep a free tier from becoming
        an open relay. Every one of these is off by default - with no keys
        in a tenant's bundle and no security requirement in its document,
        the gateway forwards exactly as phase 1, byte for byte.

        - API keys, verified in the request path against an in-memory
          table hung off the tenant. A presented key is sha256'd and
          constant-time compared against the tenant's stored hashes - the
          hash rides in the signed bundle, never the secret, and the
          compare has no early return, so which key matched (or that none
          did) is not a timing signal. The key table is NOT snapshotted
          into the compiled app: it rides in on the request from the live
          tenant meta (Maat::Router sets $env->{'maat.keys'}), so a key
          the control plane drops stops working on the next poll without
          the app being rebuilt.
        - The spec's securityScheme IS the guard. Open::API compiles
          components.securitySchemes into per-operation requirements;
          Maat registers its key check as the scheme checker Open::API
          calls (the new Open::API::check_op_security), rather than
          inventing a parallel auth path. An operation the document marks
          apiKey or http-bearer is enforced because the document says so;
          an operation with no security requirement is untouched. The
          credential is extracted by Open::API per the scheme's type and
          location (an apiKey header or query value, a bearer token), so
          one checker covers every scheme the document declares.
        - Per-(key, operation) rate limits, a fixed window on
          Maat::RateLimit. The count is process-shared across the workers
          of one gateway through an flock'd file, so a 100/min limit holds
          exact to one request across four workers rather than becoming
          400/min (t/26). It is NOT distributed across gateways: N
          gateways behind a balancer admit up to N x the limit, an honest
          number the POD states plainly rather than a fictional
          distributed limit. rate_per_min 0 is unlimited and touches
          nothing. The limiter is anchored in a Legba slot and its counts
          live in files keyed independently of the compiled spec, so a
          publish - which rebuilds a tenant's app around a fresh compiled
          object - does not reset a count (t/25 gate 2). Legba's own slots
          are copy-on-write across a fork, so Legba is used for what it is,
          a process-global anchor a registry swap does not touch, while
          the cross-process arithmetic is the flock'd file.
        - 429 in the same application/problem+json shape as a validation
          failure, with Retry-After and X-RateLimit-Limit / -Remaining /
          -Reset. A denied request carries the request id like every other
          answer.
        - Upstream credentials pass through untouched. We are not an
          identity provider: the header a key was presented in, and any
          Authorization the client sent, are forwarded to the upstream
          unchanged (t/25 asserts the upstream received both).
        - `maat revoke <key-id>` forces a revoked key to stop working at
          once rather than at the next poll. Revocation happens at the
          control plane (the key vanishes from the bundle); the data
          plane's job is to notice immediately. `maat revoke` signals the
          running gateway (SIGUSR1 by default, --pid / --pidfile) and
          Maat::Poller's installed handler forces one poll now, picking up
          the already-updated bundle within a round trip. In-process,
          Maat::Registry->revoke_key drops the key from the live table
          directly. Both are proven in t/25 gate 3, alongside the ordinary
          within-one-poll path.
        - Maat::RateLimit is new; Maat::Gateway, Maat::Registry,
          Maat::Router and Maat::Poller gained the wiring above; the C
          request path (include/mt_serve.h) runs the auth step only when a
          gateway armed it, so a keyless gateway is unchanged. Requires
          Open::API 0.09 (check_op_security) and Legba 0.08.

0.04    Unreleased
        Phases 6 and 7 of plan_maat, the data-plane half: one fleet of
        workers serves every customer's proxy, routed by hostname,
        isolated from each other, with a spec publish disturbing nobody.

        - Maat::Registry - the per-worker host -> compiled-tenant map. A
          compiled spec is DATA (Open::API->new is a runtime
          constructor), so a worker holds one entry per host and a
          publish replaces one entry without recycling anything.
          apply_bundle swaps the whole tenant table in one assignment and
          drops any entry whose digest moved; an in-flight request holds
          its own reference to the old compiled object and finishes
          against it (t/18 proves a held app keeps serving version 1
          while a fresh resolve gets version 2). Compile is LAZY - on the
          first request for a host, not at boot - and BOUNDED by an LRU
          with two caps, a compiled-spec count and a total resident-bytes
          figure; over either, the least-recently-used entry is evicted
          and its next request recompiles. Metrics are aggregate counters
          only (hits, misses, compiles, evictions, bytes_resident,
          bad_signature, bad_digest, compile_rejected, unknown_host) -
          nothing keyed by anything unbounded.
        - The off-path compile budget. A spec pathological to compile is
          rejected without holding the loop: an input over max_spec_bytes
          is refused before it is parsed (the ten-megabytes case), and a
          runaway compile is bounded by a wall-clock deadline in a forked
          child the parent can kill, since a deferred Perl alarm can never
          reach a compile spinning in C. The child limits its own address
          space (BSD::Resource when present) and only ever compiles and
          _exits, so it never touches the worker's event loop. On breach
          the host is POISONED - answered 503 until its digest changes -
          and only that tenant feels it: no neighbour's compiled entry is
          touched, so no neighbour's latency moves (t/21 kills a 30s
          compile at its 0.3s budget while a neighbour resolve stays
          sub-millisecond).
        - Maat::Poller - config distribution, poll not push, because a
          poll cannot break in a way that silently stops updating. GETs
          /registry/bundle with the ETag as If-None-Match, verifies the
          X-Maat-Bundle-Signature HMAC against the shared registry token
          BEFORE decoding, and publishes on a fresh 200. A failed poll -
          a 5xx, a timeout, a torn body, a bad signature - keeps the last
          good bundle and counts it; the registry never goes blank. The
          ETag advances only on a clean apply.
        - Maat::Router - the multi-tenant edge. Routes by Host header
          (both <slug>.openapi-proxy.com tenants and phase-7 custom
          domains) to the tenant's compiled app; an unknown host is a 404
          at the edge that never resolves a tenant or compiles a spec.
          Per-tenant isolation caps from the plan (body cap -> 413, header
          cap -> 431, concurrency ceiling -> 429) are checked before the
          tenant app is entered, and the ceiling slot is held until the
          response completes - so a tenant whose upstream hangs consumes
          at most its own ceiling and cannot cut a neighbour's throughput
          (t/21: a hung tenant pins its 4 slots and 429s the rest while a
          neighbour serves 100/100). Every response leaves with
          X-Maat-Version and X-Maat-Proxy, so under a publish each
          response is attributable to exactly one version (t/22: zero
          dropped requests through repeated republishes, no torn state).
          Per-tenant metrics are keyed by proxy id alone.
        - Phase 7, the edge half: GET /.well-known/acme-challenge/<token>
          is answered from the bundle's challenge table for any host (the
          control plane's ACME client runs the protocol; the edge only
          answers the http-01 probe). An issued domain in the bundle
          aliases its host to its proxy and its PEM cert/key are
          materialized to disk - the key mode 0600 - for Hyperman's
          tls_sni map, which loads certificates from files. A domain that
          leaves the bundle stops being served and its SNI files are
          removed within one poll (t/23 drives the whole issue -> serve ->
          remove cycle through Maat::Poller). Note: Hyperman 0.15 builds
          the SNI contexts once before forking, so a live cert swap rides
          its zero-downtime reload; the registry keeps the tls_sni map and
          the on-disk PEMs current for that reload.
        - Maat::Shard - the blast-radius function. Free and anonymous
          tenants never share a pool with paid ones; within a tier the
          pool is fnv1a_32(lc host) % N, stable across processes and
          trivially reimplementable at the edge. Maat::Registry filters a
          pooled worker's bundle to its own tenants through member(), so a
          bug in one pool cannot reach a tenant in another (t/24). The
          topology it implies is documented in the module.
        - The capacity model, measured on this machine and named:
          bench/capacity.pl and bench/CAPACITY.md. Resident bytes per
          compiled spec (8.3 KB / 77 KB / 735 KB small/median/large),
          ~20,900 median tenants per worker in a 1.5 GB budget, a 0.08 ms
          median miss, ~120,000 req/s per worker at the edge, and a
          sub-penny-per-million infrastructure cost against a named
          instance. Starter clears the 80% margin bar with orders of
          magnitude to spare.
        - Maat::Config gains a tenant() constructor - an in-memory config
          from one bundle row, no maat.yml - and Maat::Gateway accepts a
          pre-compiled api, since in the hosted plane the compile has
          already happened off the request path. enforce is downgraded to
          observe for a plan whose limits do not grant it, rather than
          answering 400 for a gateway the tenant did not pay for.

0.03    Unreleased
        Phase 4 of plan_maat, the gateway half: mock mode. A gateway
        with no upstream serves the document itself.

        - mode: mock in maat.yml. The request half is the phase-1 path
          unchanged - matched, validated, counted, and a non-conforming
          request answered 400 problem+json with its pointers, exactly
          as enforce would - which is what makes a mock a client-side
          conformance check rather than a toy. The response half is
          Open::API 0.08's synthesize: the media example, a named or the
          first examples entry, the schema default, or a value generated
          from the schema, in C, deterministic - the same request gives
          byte-identical bodies (t/17-mock.t proves 100 in a row, and
          that the served body validates against the document's own
          schema).
        - Prefer: code=404 selects a declared status and Prefer:
          example=name a named example, so error handling can be
          exercised from a client without touching the spec.
        - ?__delay=250ms (also 1.5s, or bare seconds) holds the
          response, capped at mock.max_delay (10s default): the delay
          exists to exercise timeouts, and the cap exists so a public
          mock URL cannot be held open as a DoS lever.
        - An undeclared path is 404 problem+json naming it undeclared,
          counted `undeclared` - a mock has nothing to forward the
          unknown to, and inventing a 200 for a path the document does
          not declare would defeat the one thing a mock is for.
        - MAAT_CHAOS gains a `mock` point: a synthesis that dies is
          answered 500 problem+json and counted `failed`, never a hang.
          The mock's response path fails closed with an honest error
          where the proxy's fails open with a forward, because a mock
          has nothing to fail open to. A dying validator still fails
          open onto the mock, same rule as the proxy.
        - `upstream` is refused in mode mock and required otherwise,
          both said plainly at config load; `maat check` reports the
          document as the upstream instead of probing one.
        - Needs Open::API 0.08 for synthesize.

0.03    Unreleased
        Phase 3 of plan_maat: the violation pipeline. Violations get off
        the request path immediately, aggregate into something a human
        can read, and reach the control plane without the gateway ever
        waiting for it.

        - Violations are AGGREGATED, not emitted one per failure. The
          pressure this is written against is not correctness, it is
          volume: one upstream deploy that changes a field's type turns
          every request into a violation, and a gateway that emitted a
          record for each would become the outage it was installed to
          detect. Measured at t/16: 270,000 violations became 8 records,
          with p99 unmoved (15.0us with reporting off, 18.1us on).
        - The fingerprint is (spec_version, operation_id, status,
          pointer, keyword), with array indices collapsed to `*` so an
          array of 500 bad elements is one record with count 500 rather
          than 500 records. NO VALUE FROM A PAYLOAD EVER ENTERS A KEY:
          keys are stored, indexed and displayed by the control plane,
          and nothing derived from a customer's data may end up there.
        - A parameter violation is fingerprinted as `query/limit` rather
          than by an empty pointer. Open::API reports parameter errors
          with no instanceLocation - there is no document for them to
          point into - and left as they came, `limit` being too large
          and `offset` being too large would have been one fingerprint.
        - Maat::Redact decides what may leave the process, and follows
          one rule: when in doubt, drop it. A missing example costs
          somebody a minute; an example that should not have left costs
          them a disclosure notice. Two independent tests, and a value
          must pass both - the schema (`writeOnly`, `format: password`)
          and the field's name (password, token, api_key, cvv and their
          spellings). The second exists because the first only works
          when the document is honest, and a document that forgot to
          mark its password field is exactly the one whose password
          field must not be shipped. Objects and arrays are never sent
          whole - they become `{object with 3 keys}` - because a nested
          object can hold the secret two levels down that the name test
          never saw. `redact: strict` keeps no example at all.
        - Maat::Spool: one file per closed window, bounded by count and
          bytes, written under a temporary name and renamed into place.
          Past the bound the OLDEST is deleted and counted - during an
          outage the recent violations are the ones describing what is
          happening now. A file that cannot be parsed is removed rather
          than retried forever, which would wedge everything behind it.
        - Maat::Shipper: batched, gzipped, sent with Fetch, retried with
          a backoff doubling to a five-minute ceiling. Nothing waits for
          it: the send is a future whose failure leaves the batch on the
          spool and moves a counter. Ten minutes of an unreachable
          control plane under load is a test (t/14), not a hope - the
          bound holds, the drop count is exact, and everything still
          held arrives after reconnection.
        - Fixed before it shipped: the first version of the gzip used
          Compress::Raw::Zlib with `-WindowBits => WANT_GZIP`, which
          produces a RAW DEFLATE STREAM WITH NO GZIP HEADER while we
          labelled it `Content-Encoding: gzip`. Every batch would have
          been rejected by the control plane, silently. WANT_GZIP is a
          flag to be added to the window size, not a window size - and
          `MAX_WBITS + WANT_GZIP` without parentheses parses as
          `MAX_WBITS(+WANT_GZIP())` and yields 15. It is IO::Compress::
          Gzip now, and t/14 inflates what the shipper produces rather
          than trusting its length.
        - `maat tail --violations`: the live feed on a terminal. This is
          so the product is useful before the control plane exists - a
          customer can point Maat at their API, run this in a second
          window and watch their own drift go past, with no account, no
          dashboard and no network. It reads the spool and never
          consumes it: those violations are the shipper's to send.
        - `report.metrics: /path` serves the gateway's self-report as
          JSON: the counters, response coverage, spool depth and drops,
          shipping failures and backoff. Off unless named, because it
          shadows that path on the proxied API and silently intercepting
          a path the upstream serves would be the rudest thing a
          transparent proxy could do.
        - A window is flushed by the worker's own event loop where there
          is one (armed once, from the violation path, only when
          psgix.loop says a loop is live - off a loop Hyperman->timer
          would block for the whole interval, which on a request path
          would be the worst bug in this distribution). Elsewhere the
          violation path does it. Either way the flush is under the same
          watchdog as the recorder: aggregation moved the sink call from
          record() to flush(), and the alarm moved with it, or the one
          thing this component promises - that reporting cannot hold a
          request - would have quietly stopped being true the moment it
          started batching.
        - report: { url, token, window, max_keys, environment, spool,
          spool_files, spool_bytes, interval, batch, gzip, timeout,
          redact, redact_names, metrics } in maat.yml. `window: 0`
          closes a window on every violation, for a low-traffic gateway
          that wants its drift immediately.
        - MAAT_CHAOS's `record` and `block` points now exercise the
          flush as well as the record, since that is where somebody
          else's code is now called.

0.02    Unreleased
        - The request path is XS (Maat.xs, include/mt_*.h). One C
          function, not a set of helpers: the Perl version was not slow
          line by line - measured, a counter was 0.09us and all three
          eval blocks together 0.04us - it was slow in shape, a dozen
          Perl ops and four Perl/XS crossings per request for two calls'
          worth of work. mt_serve does the request id, every counter,
          the routing, the validation, the header stamp and every branch
          between them without unwinding into Perl. The request half
          went 5.50us to 2.19us (2.5x); with response validation at the
          shipped default, 7.60us to 3.54us.
        - The six MAAT_CHAOS injection points moved into C with it, on
          purpose: a fail-open path exercised only through a Perl
          wrapper is not the path that runs in production. They fire
          inside mt_serve at the instants the real failures would.
          t/05-chaos.t is unchanged and still passes - it is the
          contract this was written against, not a description of it.
        - What deliberately stayed Perl: Open::API's router and
          validator and the proxy (real calls, now call_sv/call_method
          under G_EVAL so a die still lands in the fail-open branch);
          the violation recorder and the problem+json builder, which are
          only reached when a request has actually violated its contract
          and which own the alarm and the local $SIG{ALRM}; and
          _forward/_check_response, which own the three response shapes
          and the sampler.
        - Validation now goes through Open::API's validate_env (0.07)
          rather than validate_request: the header hash is built in C,
          for only the headers the operation declares, instead of
          walking the whole environment in Perl. That segment was 4.14us
          and is 0.66us.
        - Fixed the test client reading until EOF, which made the suite
          hang intermittently: it made the CLIENT's termination the
          server's decision, so a server keeping a connection open - as
          it may, whatever was asked - stopped the run rather than
          failing it. It now reads the declared Content-Length, with EOF
          as the fall-back and a read deadline underneath.
        - t/12-streaming.t records phase 2's second gate as UNMET, with
          a TODO rather than a lowered threshold: a 128MB body costs the
          worker 1.0x its size in RSS, where the gate asks for flat.
          PSGI's streaming writer has no backpressure signal, so nothing
          here can slow the upstream when the client is slower. The fix
          is Hyperman exposing its output queue, Fetch pausing a
          response body and Reverse::Proxy joining them; the design is
          in plan_maat/phase-2a-backpressure.md. It blocks the phase-6
          capacity model, not phase 3.

0.02    Unreleased
        Phase 2 of plan_maat: response validation and sampling. The
        product's reason to exist - every sampled response weighed
        against the schema its status declares, and a mismatch recorded
        WITHOUT ALTERING A SINGLE BYTE THE CLIENT RECEIVES.

        - Response checking on the forward path, at all three shapes a
          response comes back in: a triplet, a delayed response and a
          future. It reads the triplet and never writes to it. The gate
          is asserted byte for byte against the upstream's own answer,
          header for header, with validation on (t/11-responses.t).
        - Recorded in enforce as well as observe, and rejected in
          neither. A response cannot be un-sent, and answering 502
          because a body drifted from its schema would turn a
          documentation bug into an outage - the customer's, caused by
          us. Whether enforcement should ever extend to responses is a
          product decision, not a side effect of this one.
        - Maat::Sampler: how much to check, per operation. At most 50
          validated responses a second for each operation (the cost
          ceiling), and never longer than 10 seconds without one (so a
          quiet endpoint is covered rather than merely cheap). The budget
          is per operation on purpose: one hot endpoint must not spend it
          all and leave the other ninety uncovered. `sample: all` and
          `sample: N` are there for a staging environment and for anyone
          who wants a fixed rate.
        - $gateway->coverage reports what was actually weighed, per
          operation: total, sampled, unsampled, checked, violations and
          each skip_* - with the divisor in force. The counts add up to
          the total, and that is asserted: coverage that did not add up
          would be a guess.
        - `maat explain GET /orders/{id}` (or `maat explain getOrder`)
          prints the resolved contract - parameters, request bodies, the
          statuses that carry a schema, and what will happen to a request
          that fails - from the same compiled spec the gateway serves
          from, so the tool and the thing it describes cannot disagree.
        - responses: { validate, sample, budget, floor, interval,
          max_body } in maat.yml, and `stream: true` to forward bodies
          chunk by chunk (a streamed body is then counted skip_body and
          not validated - streaming and response validation are
          alternatives for one route, not companions).
        - MAAT_CHAOS gains a `response` point: a check that dies loses
          the sample and nothing else. The response has already been
          handed back by the time the check runs, which is why that is
          the only thing it can lose.
        - bench/gateway.pl and bench/RESULTS.md: the cost, measured. On
          one machine, per request over the bare proxy - request
          validation +0.81us, response validation at 1% +1.01us, at 100%
          +1.82us. Sampling at the default removes 80% of the cost of
          response validation.
        - That number is why two things are shaped as they are. The first
          working version added 0.39us at 1% instead of 0.20, and the
          cost was not validation: an options hashref allocated per
          request, and the whole coverage hash rebuilt per request to
          hand to a retune that runs once a second. At this scale the
          cost of asking whether to do the work can exceed the work.
        - Known, and recorded rather than hidden (t/12-streaming.t): a
          128MB body grows the worker by ~510MB buffered, ~134MB with
          `stream: true`. The response check is not holding those bytes -
          with responses.validate off the growth is identical - it is the
          forward buffering to the slower of the two sockets, in
          Reverse::Proxy and Hyperman. Left as a TODO so it un-TODOs when
          backpressure lands, rather than as a threshold loose enough to
          pass a real leak.
        - Needs Open::API 0.07 for check_response, response_coverage and
          operation_info.

0.01    Unreleased
        Phase 1 of plan_maat: the proxy core.

        - Maat::Gateway, a PSGI application that matches each request
          against a compiled OpenAPI document, validates it, and forwards
          it to the upstream through Reverse::Proxy. Nothing new is fast
          here and nothing needs to be: Open::API compiles and validates
          in C, Reverse::Proxy forwards in C through Fetch's ABI, and this
          distribution is the policy between them - which is why it has no
          XS of its own.
        - What the document does not describe is FORWARDED, in both modes,
          and counted `undeclared`. A specification that does not cover
          everything is the normal case, and a gateway that rejects what
          it was not told about cannot be installed in front of a real
          API. This is the most important default in the product.
        - observe (the default) records violations and changes nothing.
          enforce answers a non-conforming request with RFC 7807
          problem+json carrying the failing JSON pointers, and the
          upstream never sees it; a declared path with an undeclared
          method is 405 with an Allow header.
        - Fail-open is a tested property, not a claim. MAAT_CHAOS is a
          supported switch that injects the failures the request path is
          written to survive - freed (the spec handle is gone), match,
          alloc (buffering the body fails), validate, record, block (the
          reporter hangs, and a watchdog abandons it) - each with an
          optional rate. Under any of them the failure is counted and the
          traffic keeps flowing. Where only the bookkeeping broke, the
          validation verdict still stands: if a failing reporter
          downgraded enforcement, anyone who could stall the violation
          pipeline could switch off request validation.
        - Bodies are buffered to be validated and put back for the
          forward, so the upstream receives the same bytes. Over max_body
          (1MB default) nothing is read at all: the check is skipped and
          counted, and the body streams through untouched.
        - X-Request-Id: minted as a UUID v7 when the client did not bring
          one, forwarded upstream, echoed on the response and carried on
          every violation record - so a report can be found in the
          customer's own logs.
        - Maat::Config: maat.yml, validated at load. Every error names the
          key path; an unknown key is fatal with a did-you-mean, because a
          silently ignored option is a setting an operator believes is on.
          Secret references ({ $env: NAME }, { $file: path }) keep tokens
          out of files that live in repositories. A TLS listener with only
          half a certificate is refused rather than quietly serving plain
          HTTP.
        - bin/maat: `run` and `check`. check is the pre-deploy gate - it
          loads the config, compiles the spec exactly as the gateway does
          and probes the upstream, exiting non-zero with a message that
          names the fix.
        - Gate 1 (t/06-byte-identical.t): a corpus of conforming and
          undeclared requests is sent twice, once straight at the upstream
          and once through the gateway, and every response must come back
          identical - status, headers and body - minus the hop-by-hop
          headers a proxy owns and the X-Request-Id Maat adds. Putting
          Maat in front of an API may not change the API.
